    At the very beginning,what's the MOS？
    For those who commenced the study of analog electronics
,it actually evolves from a simple circuit configuration that
 calls voltage-dependent current source,which is also the basi
s of amplifier.
    At first,we need a capacitor consisting of two conductive
 plates,an insulator in the middle(dielectric)
    And we put a voltage on it called V1,as we all know, the 
final outcame is the plenty of postive charge will be attracted
to the plate connected to the postive pole.
    Negative charge,conversely,attracted and gathered together
at the bottom plate(in efffect,usually the p-type substracte
consisting of semiconductor),forming a high-density electronic
path which is called "channel"(the channel of device).
    So what will happen if we put anohter voltage V2 between 
this channel?There are a number of negative charge between the
channel,we put a voltage on it(right postive electrode,left 
negative electrode).Aha,there is a current!
    Of course ,this circuit will have the following rules:
    (a)Q increases =C * U1 increases
    If V1 increases ,Q increases,the electronic density increa
ses.(the resistence between the source and drain decreases)
    (b)If t decreases=>C increases=>Q increases
    the electronic density in the channel also increases.

The above is the initial structure that we learned.
    But we can imagine that the realistic structure is not lik
e that,it is actually a three dimensional structure.Next,we wi
ll use a diagram to illustrate it.

The imagine is produced by AI agent"chatgpt"(By the way,i don
't oppose to use agent to make explanation more concise and cl
ear).
    What's the difference between the orignal circuit and 
the realstic three dimensional model?
    The most serious problem is circuit model can't reflect th
e real physical laws of the real world.When we put an voltage
on a capacitor ,we must guarantee the V1(From now on ,they wi
ll all be referred to as VDS)over a special voltage value cal
led threshold voltage to open up the channel.
    If VDS is below this threshold voltage,we will find that 
the channel no longer exists.On the function garph of VDS-ID,
this is reflected as follows:when VDS is above the threshold,
the function rises linearly(VDS in proportion of ID);while 
when it is below this threshold,the ID remains always zero.
As long as VDS is greater than VTH(this will be abbreviation
used hereafter),the device is turned on,but the current can 
only be generated when VD(drain voltage)is greater than VS(
source voltage)(The threshold voltage is usually about 0.3 
to 0.4,in case of out regular P-type substrate).
    Well,what is the exact number of the charge in the chann
el,we need to start from the specific MOS structure of the g
ate.If we assume that the cross-sectional length of the gate
correspond to L,and the width of side is W.According to the p
rinciple of the capacitors,the number of the charge in the ch
annel conforms to the equation Q(Coulomb)=C*U=W*L*COX(the ca
pciture of the structure per unit area,per meter squarl)*VG
.But if take the threshold voltage into account,we can obtain
a equation like this Qch=W*L*COX*(VG-VTH)(overdrive voltage).
The basic equation has been established,we can then proceed to 
make deductions.
    Because the length and the width of the gate have been ass
umed,the density of charge =W*COX*(VG-VTH).Next,it is the time
to calculate the current.We need introduce a concept,namely"mo
bility" ,v=μ*E,E=-dV/dX,v:carrier drift velocity,μ:mobility,E:
electric field.In this way,we can obtain the equation for the
current in the channel(ID):ID=the density of charge *COX*v.At 
this point,we once again introduce the pressure division formula
The applied voltage has changed to VG-VTH-V(x).After moving dx
to the left side of the equation,integrate both sides separate
ly.The final formula is ID=μ*W/L*COX*((VGS-VTH)*VDS-1/2VDS(2)).
    What we need to know is that in the industrial manufactur
ing process,VGS,and VTH are generally fixed values.So,the only
parameter that can be changed in the formula is VDS.The follow
ing text is a classfication discussion based on the values of 
VDS.
    If we consider VGS as a variable,change its value,you can
observe how the overall values of the function change."lower-
lower,higher-higher",that's why we always choose a comfortab
le VGS's value.
    If we consider VDS as a variable,then the function of ID is
acutually a parabola,and from the perspective of the present time
once VGS>VTH,no matter if the ID=0,or not,the device is on.From 
this formula of parabola,we can easily noticed that when VDS=VGS
-VTH,the ID current reaches it maximum value.
    
Case One VDS<<2(VGS-VTH)
    The formula is approximated as ID=μ*W/L*COX*(VGS-VTH)*VDS,
obtaining a linear function.Just because it is a linear function
,its slope has a special meaning:I=U/R=>R=U/I,just the inverse of
our function's slope,so Ron=1/μ*W/L*COX*(VGS-VTH).Signifying the 
MOSFET is a voltage-dependent resistor(If VDS<<2(VGS-VTH)).Equally,
MOS device can also act as a switch,using comparison between VGS 
and VTH control on and off.(Like bluetooth System)

Case Two VDS>VGS-VTH
    There is a crucial concept called" pinch-off"that means the
point when VDS=VGS-VTH.At that time,the Qch while drop to zero.
Actually,we don't all know where the pinch-off is.It locates at
the part close to the right side on the channel.The negative 
charge gather at its left hand,and decreasing gradually from left
to right.On its right hand,you can't see a little bit of negative 
charge.
    Get back on track,when VDS>=VGS-VTH,there will be a pinch off,
The part on its left side is called effective L.And the function
wil conform to ID=μ*W/L*COX*(VGS-VTH).Oh!It becomes a fixed value.
So we can gradually divide the fuction into three parts.


Ⅰ VDS<<2(VGS-VTH)
linear fucction   linear region
Ⅱ VDS<=VGS-VTH
parabola function   triode region
Ⅲ  VDS>=VGS-VTH
fixed value   saturation region

